    "result_text": "All 55 tests pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures/errors), including the new address-form checkpoints Cp01, Cp12, and Cp44.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form on `POST /api/owners` while keeping the flat `address` input working.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n- **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added optional `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` properties to `OwnerFields`, updated the `address` description to note it's the composed/normalized value on responses, and dropped `address` from the `required` list (an owner may now satisfy the address requirement via either form).\n\n- **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` fields (columns `address_line1` / `address_line2`) with getters/setters. The generated DTOs pick up the new fields automatically, and MapStruct maps them.\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 compute a single canonical address up front:\n  - Normalize each supplied line with the existing `normalizeAddress` (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand `ST\u2192STREET` etc.).\n  - Prefer the structured form when `addressLine1` is non-blank; otherwise fall back to the flat `address`.\n  - The composed `address` = normalized `addressLine1`, with a single space and normalized `addressLine2` appended when line 2 is present.\n  - The required-field check flags `address` missing only when neither a non-blank `addressLine1` nor a flat `address` is supplied \u2014 so `city` is still required and earlier minimal (flat) owners remain valid.\n  - On save, store the composed `address` plus the normalized structured lines (kept null for a flat-address owner). All downstream reads (household id/hash, postcode validation, locality, duplicate message) continue to use this canonical `normalizedAddress` and the independent `city`/`postcode` fields.\n\n- **Schema DDL** (`h2`, `hsqldb`, `postgres`, `mysql`) \u2014 added the `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns. The H2 schema is the one the test suite loads.\n\nThe composed-address behavior is verified by Cp44 (`address` == `addressLine1` == `\"10 KING STREET\"`), normalization by Cp12, the either-form validity rule by Cp01, and backward compatibility by Cp44's flat-address case.",
